}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-34714/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-34714"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6651b604-6c7b-5284-8918-73ac57a5b134" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "41b83c2b-bd5e-55dd-a4ee-081ab6b03faa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "a7b2a195-a635-528c-948b-d63d4dd3d8dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "65b4743d-b76e-5065-bcd3-51eb341e107b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "70f2d002-7d01-5e72-b205-495e8b7dd009" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "548962a3-2345-5e6f-aba2-898a9d3b149c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75659/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-75659/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-75659/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-75659/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-75659/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-75659/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75659" :question/id #uuid "9f1717be-acd6-5282-be13-a9ce3bc21c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75659" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-75659/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75659"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "c811c5a6-5cd3-58bb-bf00-2384c1e614ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "409c4654-243b-5edc-a7ab-97592ad4bf18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "29d3aa6a-195d-5043-92a7-e7df6631d160" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "4333360f-20ad-542f-98ef-786b371e1808" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b6aa35f6-bc82-534e-9a99-1ac15de74659" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01d0dbba-cef8-58b8-a620-82e924074bad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75655/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-75655/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-75655/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-75655/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-75655/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-75655/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75655" :question/id #uuid "48f3ee8a-560a-5203-b938-db996454917b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75655" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75655/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75655"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "00dccb05-6434-5240-9bb8-5e7442a7d7b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "3c919a80-b519-51d5-89e1-b3492a49f913" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "0c4ebe63-1468-518b-a60c-d24200234fee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1c187017-06b7-514c-8361-b8dca95cc72c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "481e7927-dcd2-50ce-9ceb-e6341505e824" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "57eacdce-d0b1-503a-bc21-6b75907396a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-34700/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-34700/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-34700/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-34700/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-34700/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-34700/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-34700" :question/id #uuid "e9d69930-3b1b-589e-9811-11f0b215d63a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34700" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement II is false, while statements I and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-34700/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-34700"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10906"] :question/problem "The set $\\{ \\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/2fc772925dd6b793bdcb3a1eca96ea7e7f5fed17425f3500638340e047170c60.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/f73258610652de9a656f3c4ed202241c58c98f18391b1393dabe62d92d9bef7d.png)" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/210a5c50467861cdfe48b46f2098212bbf387270b420a05394e318a0e0b48c74.png)\nTo see why, notice that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/91a29b57d32ed8c8e702f2988f7714ffc343ed6b4707b86b49b708ffad5c8bc8.png)\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10907"] :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/4af8a5ea61752a394d3c3313e5cbda76ad1981d8d62573d533ece0db0dd38820.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\n- The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10908"] :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/8d88cae0e40e7a377314e359086dc45fa0460c17ed55fe379cc15537886b2623.png)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
